  "nameStory": "The 'bushy' in this plant's name comes from its dense, shrub-like growth habit that sets it apart from its more herbaceous St. John's wort relatives. The scientific name 'densiflorum' literally means 'densely flowered' in Latin, perfectly describing how this plant packs its bright yellow blooms into thick, crowded clusters. Like other St. John's worts, it's named after St. John the Baptist, as these plants traditionally bloom around his feast day in late June.",
  "description": "Bushy St. John's wort is a compact, woody perennial that grows more like a small shrub than the typical herb-like St. John's worts most people know. It reaches about 2-4 feet tall and forms dense, rounded clumps with many branching stems. The plant produces masses of small, bright yellow flowers clustered tightly together in summer, creating a showy golden display. Its leaves are small and narrow, giving the whole plant a fine-textured, almost delicate appearance despite its sturdy woody nature.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Bright golden-yellow flowers packed densely into rounded, flat-topped clusters that feel almost cushion-like to the touch when you gently press them",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Forms a compact, bushy mound with multiple woody stems from the base, much more shrub-like than the single-stemmed herbs of most St. John's worts",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Small, narrow leaves that feel smooth and slightly waxy, arranged in opposite pairs along the stems with a fine, delicate texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stems",
      "description": "Multiple woody, branching stems that feel firm and don't bend easily like the softer stems of herbaceous St. John's worts",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "In New England, bushy St. John's wort prefers well-drained, sandy or rocky soils in open, sunny locations. You're most likely to find it along roadsides, in old fields, or on disturbed ground where the soil doesn't stay too wet. It thrives in areas with good drainage and full sun exposure.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New growth emerges as small, bright green leaves on woody stems. The plant begins to fill out into its characteristic bushy shape.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming season brings dense clusters of bright yellow flowers from mid to late summer. This is when the plant is most easily identified and most showy.",
    "fall": "Flowers fade and small seed capsules develop. Leaves may turn slightly yellow before dropping, revealing the woody stem structure.",
    "winter": "The woody stems persist above ground, appearing as a small, branched shrub skeleton that helps distinguish it from herbaceous St. John's worts that die back completely."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being one of the rarest St. John's worts in New England with only 9 recorded observations, bushy St. John's wort is actually quite common in the southeastern United States, making our New England populations special northern outposts of a primarily southern species!",
  "confusionNotes": "The woody, shrub-like growth habit is your best clue to separate this from other St. John's worts, which are typically soft-stemmed herbs. Remember: 'bushy' means woody stems that don't die back in winter, while most other St. John's worts disappear completely above ground.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "This species represents the northern edge of its range in New England, which explains why it's so rarely encountered here. Climate change may potentially allow it to become more common in our region over time, making current sightings particularly noteworthy for tracking range shifts.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike the common St. John's wort which is a tall, herbaceous plant with large leaves, or the tiny pineweed which is thread-like and delicate, bushy St. John's wort stands out with its woody, shrub-like structure. While shrubby St. John's-wort is also woody, bushy St. John's wort has much denser flower clusters and smaller, narrower leaves. The dense, cushion-like flower clusters are really the giveaway feature that separates it from all its relatives.",
